1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,384
One bicycle ride to college.

2
00:00:02,384 --> 00:00:04,733
How much maths can hide inside it?

3
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:08,155
Aarav counts four friends
waiting at the gate.

4
00:00:08,155 --> 00:00:10,768
He rides at fifteen kilometres an hour.

5
00:00:10,768 --> 00:00:13,700
He leans into a turn
of forty five degrees.

6
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:18,059
And when a friend tosses him a
ball, it flies in a perfect curve.

7
00:00:18,509 --> 00:00:20,902
A count, a speed, an angle, a curve.

8
00:00:20,902 --> 00:00:24,773
This year, you learn the tools to
describe every one of them exactly.

9
00:00:25,423 --> 00:00:28,104
So what is Class 11 maths really about?

10
00:00:28,454 --> 00:00:31,079
It is not just about doing sums faster.

11
00:00:31,079 --> 00:00:33,146
It is a language for patterns.

12
00:00:33,546 --> 00:00:37,139
Learn the words and the rules,
and every chapter opens up.

13
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:40,045
Here is the map of the whole year.

14
00:00:40,045 --> 00:00:42,282
Fourteen chapters in the NCERT book.

15
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:44,917
This playlist starts with the first four.

16
00:00:45,317 --> 00:00:46,659
Chapter one, sets.

17
00:00:46,659 --> 00:00:50,271
How to group things, so that
everyone agrees what belongs.

18
00:00:50,621 --> 00:00:53,070
Chapter two, relations and functions.

19
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:55,539
Rules that link one thing to another.

20
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:58,563
Chapter three, trigonometric functions.

21
00:00:58,563 --> 00:01:00,448
Turns, angles and waves.

22
00:01:00,798 --> 00:01:04,338
Chapter four, complex numbers
and quadratic equations.

23
00:01:04,338 --> 00:01:07,112
Numbers that live beyond the number line.

24
00:01:07,762 --> 00:01:10,221
Before we start, let's check your toolkit.

25
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:12,375
The first tool is the number system.

26
00:01:12,775 --> 00:01:15,115
Natural numbers are the counting numbers.

27
00:01:15,115 --> 00:01:17,323
One, two, three, and on forever.

28
00:01:17,723 --> 00:01:20,971
Add zero and the negatives,
and you get the integers.

29
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:25,080
Add every fraction, p over
q, where q is not zero.

30
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:27,453
Now you have the rational numbers.

31
00:01:27,853 --> 00:01:31,145
But some numbers can never
be written as a fraction.

32
00:01:31,145 --> 00:01:32,045
Root two.

33
00:01:31,927 --> 00:01:32,827
Pi.

34
00:01:32,366 --> 00:01:34,465
These are the irrational numbers.

35
00:01:34,865 --> 00:01:38,666
Rationals and irrationals together
fill the whole number line.

36
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:41,021
Together they make the real numbers.

37
00:01:41,671 --> 00:01:44,015
Each family sits inside the next.

38
00:01:44,015 --> 00:01:46,463
Every natural number is an integer.

39
00:01:46,463 --> 00:01:48,320
Every integer is rational.

40
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:50,021
Every rational is real.

41
00:01:50,771 --> 00:01:54,181
How can you tell a rational
number from its decimal?

42
00:01:54,181 --> 00:01:55,081
Watch.

43
00:01:55,184 --> 00:01:57,864
One quarter is zero point two five.

44
00:01:57,864 --> 00:01:59,336
The decimal stops.

45
00:01:59,736 --> 00:02:03,499
One third is zero point three
three three, repeating forever.

46
00:02:03,499 --> 00:02:05,625
It never stops, but it repeats.

47
00:02:06,025 --> 00:02:10,414
Root two is one point four one four
two, and the digits never stop and

48
00:02:10,414 --> 00:02:11,398
never repeat.

49
00:02:11,398 --> 00:02:14,080
That is the sign of an irrational number.

50
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:15,595
Your turn.

51
00:02:15,595 --> 00:02:18,951
Zero point one two one
two one two, repeating.

52
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:20,613
Rational or irrational?

53
00:02:20,613 --> 00:02:21,907
Pause and think.

54
00:02:25,107 --> 00:02:26,007
Rational!

55
00:02:25,853 --> 00:02:28,121
The block one two repeats forever.

56
00:02:28,121 --> 00:02:30,703
In fact it is twelve over ninety nine.

57
00:02:31,353 --> 00:02:34,253
The second tool is the
language of unknowns.

58
00:02:34,603 --> 00:02:36,624
A letter like x is a variable.

59
00:02:36,624 --> 00:02:39,372
It stands for a number we do not know yet.

60
00:02:39,772 --> 00:02:42,819
An equation says two
things are exactly equal.

61
00:02:42,819 --> 00:02:45,614
x plus three equals seven has one answer.

62
00:02:45,614 --> 00:02:46,549
x is four.

63
00:02:46,949 --> 00:02:50,219
An inequality says one
side is smaller or bigger.

64
00:02:50,219 --> 00:02:52,510
x plus three is less than seven.

65
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:55,195
Now every number less than four works.

66
00:02:55,195 --> 00:02:58,539
Not one answer, but a whole
stretch of the number line.

67
00:02:59,189 --> 00:03:00,257
Remember this.

68
00:03:00,257 --> 00:03:01,745
Maths is a language.

69
00:03:01,745 --> 00:03:04,769
Learn the words, and
every chapter opens up.

70
00:03:05,469 --> 00:03:10,058
The third tool is a short list of
symbols you will see in every episode.

71
00:03:10,408 --> 00:03:12,741
This curly symbol means belongs to.

72
00:03:12,741 --> 00:03:15,073
Two belongs to the natural numbers.

73
00:03:15,473 --> 00:03:17,197
The arrow means implies.

74
00:03:17,197 --> 00:03:20,146
If x is four, then x squared is sixteen.

75
00:03:20,546 --> 00:03:24,904
And the two straight bars mean the
size of a number, ignoring its sign.

76
00:03:24,904 --> 00:03:26,946
The size of minus five is five.

77
00:03:27,596 --> 00:03:30,490
Every episode in this
playlist runs the same way.

78
00:03:30,890 --> 00:03:32,603
A puzzle from daily life.

79
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:35,049
The idea, built with a live picture.

80
00:03:35,049 --> 00:03:37,200
A worked example, step by step.

81
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,032
Then the exam part.

82
00:03:39,032 --> 00:03:43,942
The common trap, an exam level question,
and a recap card for your notes.

83
00:03:44,592 --> 00:03:46,328
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:46,678 --> 00:03:49,326
Sort these six numbers
into their families.

85
00:03:49,326 --> 00:03:53,317
Minus five, zero, three quarters,
root two, pi, and seven.

86
00:03:53,817 --> 00:03:54,717
Step one.

87
00:03:54,632 --> 00:03:57,752
Seven is a counting
number, so it is natural.

88
00:03:57,752 --> 00:04:00,614
It is also an integer, rational and real.

89
00:04:01,364 --> 00:04:02,264
Step two.

90
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:06,345
Minus five and zero are integers,
but not natural numbers.

91
00:04:07,095 --> 00:04:07,995
Step three.

92
00:04:07,956 --> 00:04:12,290
Three quarters is a fraction, so
it is rational, but not an integer.

93
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:13,940
Step four.

94
00:04:13,931 --> 00:04:17,146
Root two and pi cannot
be written as fractions.

95
00:04:17,146 --> 00:04:19,716
They are irrational, but still real.

96
00:04:20,366 --> 00:04:21,831
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:22,181 --> 00:04:25,326
Many students write: pi
equals twenty two over seven.

98
00:04:25,726 --> 00:04:27,270
But pi is irrational.

99
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:32,335
Twenty two over seven is only a close
guess, three point one four two eight.

100
00:04:32,335 --> 00:04:37,040
Pi is three point one four one five
nine, and its digits never repeat.

101
00:04:37,690 --> 00:04:39,269
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:39,269 --> 00:04:40,583
Not on its own.

103
00:04:40,583 --> 00:04:44,612
These tools sit inside almost
every JEE and board question.

104
00:04:45,212 --> 00:04:46,453
Exam level now.

105
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:49,536
How many of these five
numbers are rational?

106
00:04:49,536 --> 00:04:50,830
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:57,406
The answer is C.

108
00:04:57,406 --> 00:04:58,306
Three.

109
00:04:58,463 --> 00:05:02,200
Root two times root eight is
root sixteen, which is four.

110
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:03,100
Rational.

111
00:05:03,436 --> 00:05:07,343
Root three plus one, squared,
is four plus two root three.

112
00:05:07,343 --> 00:05:10,305
Take away two root
three, and four is left.

113
00:05:10,305 --> 00:05:11,205
Rational.

114
00:05:11,546 --> 00:05:14,641
One point two repeating
is eleven over nine.

115
00:05:14,641 --> 00:05:15,541
Rational.

116
00:05:15,944 --> 00:05:21,014
Pi minus twenty two over seven is
irrational, and the non repeating decimal

117
00:05:21,014 --> 00:05:22,173
is irrational.

118
00:05:22,173 --> 00:05:23,896
So three are rational.

119
00:05:24,496 --> 00:05:25,958
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:25,958 --> 00:05:27,461
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:30,661 --> 00:05:33,659
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:34,059 --> 00:05:34,959
One.

123
00:05:34,630 --> 00:05:37,213
Is zero point seven five rational?

124
00:05:37,213 --> 00:05:39,070
Write it as p over q.

125
00:05:39,970 --> 00:05:40,870
Two.

126
00:05:40,463 --> 00:05:44,748
What is the difference between x
equals three and x less than three?

127
00:05:45,648 --> 00:05:46,548
Three.

128
00:05:46,262 --> 00:05:48,590
Is every integer a natural number?

129
00:05:49,690 --> 00:05:54,162
Next time: three friends, three different
lists, and one list that everyone

130
00:05:54,162 --> 00:05:55,062
agrees on.

131
00:05:54,969 --> 00:05:57,158
Our first chapter begins with sets.
